---
<Docs
  title="Getting started"
  description="Build Pencil from source, render a frame, compare the GPU with the CPU, export a video and run figures in a browser."
  path="/docs/getting-started/"
  headings={headings}
>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Pencil is built from source for now: there are no releases, and its package isn’t published.
    Everything below runs from a checkout of the repository.
  </p>
  <p>
    To try it with nothing installed, open the <a href="/playground/">playground</a>, which runs in
    the browser.
  </p>
  <p class="status">
    The syntax and the compiled form change freely while the project is young, so a figure written
    today may need edits later.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-you-need">What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://rustup.rs">Rust</a>, for everything.</li>
    <li>The <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> target, only to draw figures in a browser.</li>
    <li><code>ffmpeg</code>, only for video.</li>
    <li>Python 3, only for <code>scripts/serve.py</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>git clone {REPO}.git
cd pencil-lang</code></pre>

  <h2 id="render-a-frame">Render a frame</h2>
  <p>
    A figure is a <code>.pen</code> document, drawn with one or more <code>.style</code> sheets in
    order. <code>--t</code> picks a moment in seconds.
  </p>
  <pre><code>cargo run --release -p pencil-cli -- render corpus/012-limacon/limacon.pen \
  --style corpus/012-limacon/plain.style --t 1 --out out/limacon.png</code></pre>
  <p>
    The same document takes a different sheet without changing. The breathing circle, banded by
    distance:
  </p>
  <pre><code>cargo run --release -p pencil-cli -- render corpus/001-circle/circle.pen \
  --style corpus/001-circle/bands.style --t 1.25 --out out/frame.png</code></pre>

  <h2 id="gpu-against-cpu">Compare the GPU with the CPU</h2>
  <p>
    Every figure has two renderers: shaders on the GPU, and a simple CPU interpreter that defines
    what each pixel should be. <code>check</code> draws both and fails if they differ by more than a
    few levels in any channel.
  </p>
  <pre><code>cargo run --release -p pencil-cli -- check corpus/012-limacon/limacon.pen \
  --style corpus/012-limacon/plain.style</code></pre>

  <h2 id="video">Export a video</h2>
  <p>
    Video is the same figure with time running. <code>--size</code> is the width in pixels.
  </p>
  <pre><code>cargo run --release -p pencil-cli -- video corpus/003-unroll/unroll.pen \
  --style corpus/003-unroll/blog.style --size 1280 --out out/unroll.mp4</code></pre>

  <h2 id="browser">Figures in a browser</h2>
  <p>
    The core compiles to WebAssembly, and <code>&lt;pencil-figure&gt;</code>, in the package at
    <code>packages/figure</code>, draws a figure on a page with WebGPU, or WebGL2 where the
    browser has no WebGPU. The first build installs a matching <code>wasm-bindgen</code> and takes a
    few minutes.
  </p>
  <pre><code>rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown
scripts/build-web.sh
scripts/serve.py</code></pre>
  <p>
    Then open <code>http://localhost:8741/web/playground.html</code>. <code>web/</code> has a page
    for most figures, and adding <code>?gl</code> to a page’s address uses WebGL2.
  </p>
  <p>On a page of your own, the element needs only the figure and its sheets:</p>
  <pre><code>&lt;script type="module" src="packages/figure/src/element.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;pencil-figure src="corpus/012-limacon/limacon.pen"
  sheets="corpus/012-limacon/plain.style"&gt;&lt;/pencil-figure&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    It starts the GPU only when the figure comes near the viewport, and lets it go when the figure
    leaves the page. Its attributes, methods and events are described at the top of
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/packages/figure/src/element.js`}><code>packages/figure/src/element.js</code></a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A site with a bundler imports the package, <code>@pencil-lang/figure</code>. It isn’t published
    yet, so it is installed from a checkout. In Astro its <code>&lt;Figure&gt;</code> compiles each
    figure at build and ships it with a poster, which shows until the figure's first frame and is
    what a reader without JavaScript or a GPU sees. Its
    <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/packages/figure`}>README</a> has both.
  </p>

  <h2 id="latex">Bring formulas from LaTeX</h2>
  <p>
    Formulas copied from Desmos or MathLive, one per line, come back as Pencil text:
  </p>
  <pre><code>cargo run --release -p pencil-cli -- latex formulas.tex</code></pre>
  <p>The playground does the same when LaTeX is pasted into it.</p>

  <h2 id="tests">Run the tests</h2>
  <pre><code>cargo test --workspace</code></pre>
  <p>
    Each figure in <code>corpus/</code> has its expected compiled form beside it. After a change that
    is meant to alter it, write the new ones:
  </p>
  <pre><code>UPDATE_GOLDENS=1 cargo test -p pencil-core --test corpus</code></pre>

  <h2 id="pencil-command">The pencil command</h2>
  <pre><code>pencil compile DOC.pen [--style FILE]... --out FIGURE.pfig
pencil build  DOC.pen [--style FILE]... [--t SECONDS] [--param NAME=VALUE]... [--size WIDTH] [--card] --out DIR
pencil ir     DOC.pen
pencil wgsl   DOC.pen [--style FILE]...
pencil render DOC.pen [--style FILE]... [--t SECONDS] [--param NAME=VALUE]... [--size WIDTH] [--cpu] --out FRAME.png
pencil check  DOC.pen [--style FILE]... [--t SECONDS]... [--param NAME=VALUE]... [--size WIDTH] [--out DIR]
pencil video  DOC.pen [--style FILE]... [--size WIDTH] [--fps N] [--seconds S] [--font FILE] --out MOVIE.mp4
pencil latex  FILE.tex...
pencil help</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>cargo run --release -p pencil-cli --</code> stands in for <code>pencil</code> in a checkout.
    <code>ir</code> prints a figure’s compiled form, and <code>wgsl</code> the shader it becomes.
    <code>compile</code> writes a figure and its sheets as one file a page loads without parsing, and
    <code>build</code> writes that with its poster, which is what the package runs for a site.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">Where next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The <a href="/docs/language/">language reference</a>, for the statements, built-ins and styles.</li>
    <li>The <a href="/demos/">demos</a>, for every figure in the corpus with its source.</li>
    <li>The <a href="/playground/">playground</a>, to edit one and watch it recompile.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
